<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Ligne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Ligne is a noble family of Hainaut, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, named after the village of Ligne near Ath and recorded from the 11th century. Its members served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> as soldiers and diplomats, and in the 16th century a branch inherited the lordship of Arenberg through marriage and took the Arenberg name. Its best-known member is the soldier and writer Charles-Joseph, Prince de Ligne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-ligne.jpg" alt="House of Ligne" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AtlanteanAstorian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_of_Ligne.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Counted among the oldest noble families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the house takes its name from the village of Ligne, between Ath and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> in the present-day province of Hainaut. Its rise in the nobility was gradual: barons in the 12th century, counts of Fauquemberg and princes of Épinoy in the 16th century, then princes of Amblise in 1608. Its arms are blazoned Or a bend gules.</p><p>The family also gave several abbots and abbesses. Gérard de Ligne, who died in 1270, was abbot of Cambrai, and women of the family were abbesses of Epinlieu, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and of Cambrai; Catherine de Ligne, who died in 1581, was abbess of Thorn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family became imperial counts on 18 December 1544. On 20 March 1601 Emperor Rudolf II made Lamoral I a Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> as Prince de Ligne, a title extended to all his agnatic descendants, male and female. When the Imperial County of Ligne was lost through the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>, the family received in 1803 the secularised imperial abbey of Edelstetten, with a vote in the Imperial College of Princes. It sold Edelstetten to Prince Nikolaus Esterházy on 22 May 1804, two years before the Holy Roman Empire was abolished.</p><p>Cadet branches of the house included Barbançon, Barbançon-Arenberg, Moÿ, Ham, Arenberg and La Trémoïlle. Charles-Joseph held the title of 7th Prince from 1766 to 1814 and was followed by Eugène, 8th Prince from 1814 to 1880. In 1923 the Belgian Crown confirmed the style of Highness for all members of the surviving branches on 31 May, and on 22 October recognised the titles of Prince d'Amblise and Prince d'Epinoy for the head of the house.</p><p>In the 20th century the family married into several reigning houses. Yolande, daughter of the 11th Prince, became Archduchess Yolande of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the daughter-in-law of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-i">Charles I of Austria</a>. Antoine, 13th Prince from 1985 to 2005, married Alix, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-grand-duchess-of-luxembourg">Charlotte, Grand Duchess of Luxembourg</a>, and Felix of Bourbon-Parma.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Ligne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The lordship of Arenberg, named after a castle in the Eifel, passed through heiresses to the <b>Ligne</b> family in the 16th century, which took the Arenberg name.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-arenberg">Duchy of Arenberg</a></li></ul></section>
